      "evidence_type": "published tag insertion at this exact site",
      "functional_impact_measured": "Non-permeabilized antibody labeling confirmed extracellular surface display of the FLAG tag; the 3F-NHE6-HA construct was used as a functional tool to study endosomal pH regulation and NHE6 trafficking \u2014 function preserved.",
      "confidence": "high",
      "rationale": "Residue 53 = M, residue 54 = D, matching the paper's explicit statement 'after residue Met 53'. Position 53 falls within the first extracellular span (EC1 = 43\u201370), 11 residues from the signal-peptide cleavage site (SP ends at 42) and 18 residues from the TM1 boundary (TM1 starts at 71), both well above the \u22653-residue minimum. The tag is inserted into the mature extracellular protein, not within the cleaved signal peptide. A triple FLAG epitope (AAA-DYKDDDDK-G-DYKDDDDK-G-DYKDDDDK-AAA) was inserted into full-length, membrane-anchored NHE6 immediately after Met53 in the first extracellular loop, generating the 3F-NHE6-HA construct. The construct was expressed in mammalian cells, the FLAG tag was accessible to antibody without permeabilization (confirming extracellular surface display), and the tagged protein was used as a functional tool to study endosomal pH regulation and trafficking. [validation: surface_and_function; position: validated; source: paper; entailment_verified: True]",
      "sources": [
        {
          "pmid": 27590723,
          "citation": "PMID 27590723",
          "claim": "The same template (NHE6 HA ) was also used to introduce a triple Flag epitope (AAA DYKDDDDK G DYKDDDDK G DYKDDDDK AAA) in the first extracellular loop immediately after residue Met 53 ."
        }
      ],
